**Print 1 to n without a loop.** Use recursion:

```c
void print(int i, int n) {
    if (i > n) return;
    printf("%d ", i);
    print(i + 1, n);
}
```

Call `print(1, n)`. Time O(n); stack depth O(n), so a very large n overflows the stack. C++ alternatives: template recursion at compile time, or `std::iota` plus `std::for_each` (loops hidden in the library, which the interviewer may not accept).

**Memory leak.** Heap memory that is no longer reachable but was never freed, so it cannot be reused for the life of the process.

```c
void f(void) { int *p = malloc(100); p = NULL; }   // leaked
```

Other causes: early returns before `free`, error paths, lost pointer after `realloc` failure, cycles with reference counting. Avoid by: pairing every allocation with one owner and one free, single exit or `goto cleanup`, RAII and smart pointers in C++ (`unique_ptr`), and detection tools (Valgrind, AddressSanitizer/LeakSanitizer).

**Binary search complexity proof.** Each step compares with the middle and discards half, so T(n) = T(n/2) + c with T(1) = c. Unrolling k times: T(n) = T(n/2^k) + kc. The range is down to 1 when 2^k = n, so k = log2 n and T(n) = c log2 n + c = O(log n). Equivalently, a decision tree with n leaves has height at least log2 n, so any comparison search needs Omega(log n) in the worst case.

**Linked list?** Not suitable. Finding the middle node costs O(n) traversal, so the total is O(n) (the sum n/2 + n/4 + ... is still O(n)), no better than linear search; only the comparison count remains O(log n). Binary search needs O(1) random access (arrays). For lists use linear search, or switch to a skip list or balanced BST for O(log n).
